  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nSaturday, the region is forecast to be under a weak upper-level flow \ncontinuing the warming trend. High temperatures are forecast in the \n90s to 100s. There is a chance for scattered afternoon thunderstorms \ndue to embedded shortwave passages and a surface low forecast to \ndevelop over the Tri-State area. Probability of Precipation (PoPs) \nare around 20%. If storms are able to form, large hail and strong \nwinds are the primary hazards.\n\nBeginning Sunday, we transition to more of a zonal upper-level flow \nwith a ridge building west of the Rocky Mountains. High temperatures \nare forecast to remain unseasonably warm for the county warning area \n(CWA) through Tuesday with highs in the 90s to 100s. A weak cold \nfront moving through Tuesday night will bring modest cooling, but \nhigh temperatures are still forecast in the 90s for Wednesday and \nThursday. \n\nStarting Monday evening through at least Wednesday evening, there is \na chance for showers and thunderstorms overnight due to embedded \nshortwave passages. Late Wednesday, we begin to transition to more \nof a northwest upper-level flow with increased upper-level support \nfrom a jet streak overhead. This will bring chances for showers and \nthunderstorms overnight Wednesday through the remainder of the \nforecast period. Best chances for showers and thunderstorms will be \nin the late evenings and overnight hours Wednesday and Thursday \nwith PoPs around 50-70%. \n\nDespite the unseasonably warm temperatures, fire weather concerns \nare minimal for the forecast period. Relative humidity (RH) values \nfor the western CWA are forecast in the low teens to 20s for \nSaturday through Monday. Peak wind gusts are forecast to be around \n25 mph, but highest wind gusts do not coincide with lowest RH values \nof the day. Beginning Monday afternoon, winds will primarily be from \nthe east advecting additional moisture into the Tri-State area \nallowing minimum RH values to gradually recover into the 20s to 30s \nafter Tuesday.",
